#### 1. Oversized Struct With Redundant Precomputed Fields (1 instance)
**Impact:** At 48 bytes (1 `long` + 5 `double`), this struct is 3× the recommended 16-byte max. Every arithmetic operator (`+`, `-`, `*`, `/`) constructs a new `DataSize`, running 5 floating-point divisions and copying 48 bytes. With thousands of throughput calculations per second, this dominates.
**Files:** `DataSize.cs:L42-50` (constructor), `L53-68` (properties), `L124-127` (operators)
**Fix:** Store only `Bits` (8 bytes). Compute `Bytes`, `Kilobytes`, etc. as `readonly` computed properties (`=> Bits / 8.0`). This shrinks the struct to 8 bytes and eliminates 5 divisions per construction — paying only for the unit actually accessed.

```csharp
// Before: 48 bytes, 5 divisions per construction
public DataSize(double byteSize)
{
    Bits = (long)Math.Ceiling(byteSize * BitsInByte);
    Bytes = byteSize;
    Kilobytes = byteSize / BytesInKilobyte;
    Megabytes = byteSize / BytesInMegabyte;
    Gigabytes = byteSize / BytesInGigabyte;
    Terabytes = byteSize / BytesInTerabyte;
}

// After: 8 bytes, 0 divisions per construction
public DataSize(double byteSize)
{
    Bits = (long)Math.Ceiling(byteSize * BitsInByte);
}

public long Bits { get; }
public double Bytes => (double)Bits / BitsInByte;
public double Kilobytes => Bytes / BytesInKilobyte;
